WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.654
How do you simplify a matrix to reveal its
rank? You can solve simultaneous equations

00:05.654 --> 00:11.308
by elimination. In this lesson we use
elementary row operations to find rank and

00:11.308 --> 00:16.963
solve systems. By the end, Use row
operations, find rank and solve systems.

00:16.963 --> 00:20.857
The three elementary row operations are
swapping rows, scaling a row, and adding a

00:20.857 --> 00:24.752
multiple of one row to another.

00:24.752 --> 00:27.655
Subtracting twice the first row from the
second gives a zero row, so the rank is

00:27.655 --> 00:30.557
one.

00:30.557 --> 00:34.228
The rank is the number of non-zero rows in
row echelon form, which equals the number

00:34.228 --> 00:37.899
of independent rows.

00:37.899 --> 00:42.764
A common mistake is to change only one
entry when scaling a row. Pause and decide

00:42.764 --> 00:47.629
whether that is right. Apply the operation
to the whole row.

00:47.629 --> 00:51.609
Your turn. Find the rank of one two three
six. Pause the video. Hint: The second row

00:51.609 --> 00:55.589
is three times the first.

00:55.589 --> 01:00.103
The rank is one. In a new context, Rank is
the number of non-zero rows in echelon

01:00.103 --> 01:04.616
form. Rate your confidence from one to
five.

01:04.616 --> 01:08.916
Row reduce to echelon form, then count the
non-zero rows to get the rank. Next, we

01:08.916 --> 01:13.216
find inverses by row reduction.
